cspan's american history tv continues  now you can find the full schedule for  the weekend on your program guide or at  cspan.org  history  flying west across the pacific vice  president and mrs richard nixon began  their tour of six nations that was to  take them completely around the world  their first destination was manila  the busy capital of the philippines was  like the rest of the country tense with  excitement on the eve of a great  national celebration  the 10th anniversary of independence  [Music]  on the afternoon of july 3rd mr and mrs  nixon landed at international airport  vice president carlos garcia of the  republic of the philippines was there to  welcome them  high-ranking members of the philippine  government of congress and of the armed  forces also extended their greetings  the warmth of the reception was  everywhere  this was more than just a visit of state  by the second highest official of the us  government  it was a gesture of the mutual esteem  between americans and filipinos  several times along dewey boulevard the  visitor halted the cavalcade to  acknowledge the tremendous cordiality  shown him  brief ceremonies outside malacanang  palace mr garcia accompanied the honored  guests to where president and mrs ramon  magsaysay awaited them  this again was more than a mere visit it  was a warm reunion between old friends  on the morning of the 4th of july a day  glorious to both filipinos and americans  mr nixon later wreath at the monument of  jose rizal  the filipino patriot and martyr whose  name is known to millions who cherish  freedom  the solemn moment brought memories to  all present for it was exactly ten years  earlier on this very site that the late  president manuel rojas had first raised  the flag of the new philippines republic  [Music]  once more the innate friendliness of the  u.s vice president was expressed to the  many many people who'd come to see and  to greet him  [Music]  [Applause]  but now the moment for the greatest  event had arrived  over a half million people were gathered  at lunetta park when president and mrs  magsize joined ex-president sergio  osmena and mr and mrs nixon to review a  mammoth parade  this was the exuberant climax of the  nationwide celebration of this tenth  year of independence  in the two-hour long procession more  than 50 huge floats rolled by  they depicted philippine progress in the  past decade and they pointed to the  future goals of this country a country  that not so long ago was left crushed  and shattered by war  [Music]  overhead planes spelled out philippine  independence 10 years  and on the ground military units marched  smartly in review  [Music]  every branch of the island republic's  armed services took part in this giant  demonstration of patriotic spirits  [Music]  by permission of president max heisei  the huge crowd was allowed to surge  forward at the parade's end the better  to hear the address of vice president  nixon  i have witnessed many fourth of july  parades  i have seen parades with  many floats and many bands  but in all my life i have never seen a  parade to equal the one the philippines  put on today  we in the united states in our early  years had the problem  of our tories  who were still loyal to a foreign ruler  but your problem in the philippines  was a far greater one  the authority of your government was  challenged by the hawks  now many of these rebels  as you know  were simple people  probably the great majority of them were  but they were led astray  by their  leaders but you also know  that among the leaders were men  who were not truly filipinos  because they owed their first allegiance  not to the philippine government but to  the materialistic ruthless foreign  ideology of communist colonial  imperialism  and what did you do about it  and this to me is one of the great  stories of this republic  and one that should be told and retold  all over the world  it's true that you used military force  where necessary in dealing with the hux  but  you also did something  perhaps even more important  you removed the political and economic  causes of discontent  and in this effort  your president  raymond magsaysai  played a leading part and for this  believe me  president magsaysay has won the  admiration of the whole free world  i know that there are those  who feel that friendly neutrality  towards the kremlin  and p ping  may spare them  but you know there is an old proverb  and it is that he who stops with the  devil  must have a long spoon  the communists have been ruthless  toward the people of the nations they  have engulfed  they have no memory of former favors  no kindness toward those who tried to be  friendly  they are cold and calculating mastery  and those who feel that they can out  maneuver them  are taking a fearful risk  we hope that all the nations of asia  will understand  our attitude toward collective security  and may i say at this point that  i appreciate particularly the fact that  with the rain coming down so many of you  still will stand there and listen as we  finish our speech  may i thank you for listening so  patiently  and may i say to you  that as was the case three years ago  mrs nixon and i  have been deeply touched by the welcome  that we have received all over your  country  i trust  that the years ahead  we'll see the republic of the  philippines make even greater progress  in the next 10 years than in the last 10  my boo  next day president magsaysai and vice  president nixon visited corregidor  its long silent guns  remain a memorial to the brave men who  defended this island fortress  corregidor is a bond between two nations  for it was here that filipinos and  americans fought and died side by side  malenta tunnel was also toured by the  leaders who shared vivid impressions of  its historic past  it was here that living quarters had  been provided for the first philippines  president manuel quezon  and it was here the generals macarthur  and wainwright planned their strategy  president magsaisai arranged this trip  at the request of mr nixon who wished to  see for himself the hardships that the  defenders of democracy had experienced  [Music]  next day manila's national press club  specially adorned for the visitors from  washington became the vice president's  final stop before he flew to saigon  this too provided a reunion with men  whom he hadn't seen in three years  during a half-hour interview he  impressed the news men by his frankness  in discussing controversial issues  of particular interest to them was his  assurance that title to us military  bases would be returned to the  philippines  [Music]  later that morning in the wake of a  monsoon rain vice president and mrs  nixon arrived in the capital of south  vietnam  u.s ambassador frederick reinhardt  representatives of president ziem and a  throng of vietnamese citizens greeted  them  in no other place were the nixons to see  a greater change since their previous  visit to asia  then this land had been in the throes of  war  today it was a free and peaceful  republic  while riding into saigon signs over the  road testified to the friendship vietnam  feels toward the american people and  their elected representatives  this happened to be a time of national  rejoicing  everywhere there were people carrying  banners marching in celebration of the  second anniversary of engojin gems  presidency  thousands gathered on the spacious lawn  before the presidential palace to cheer  the republic and president in godzilla  jim when mr nixon came to be received by  the young nation's chief executive  [Music]  the two leaders conferred for an hour  and a half mr nixon extended president  eisenhower's warmest personal  congratulations well earned by a man and  his people who had shown astonishing  progress and spirit through two  difficult years  [Music]  at president diem's invitation the  visitor from washington joined him in  facing the vast assemblage outside  speaking extemporaneously to his  cheering countrymen  jim thanked them for their splendid  unity which he feels has been  responsible for the success their nation  has achieved  he also thanked the united states  through its vice president for its help  not only to vietnam but to the entire  free world  in response mr nixon told his hosts that  america was proud indeed to be vietnam's  friend and alive  another high point occurred at the  vietnamese national constituent assembly  the u.s vice president became the first  guest speaker ever invited to appear  before this body for mr nixon this  privilege had special significance for  back in america he'd served many years  as a legislator  to the representatives of the vietnamese  people he expressed his admiration for  the accomplishments that have marked the  first two years of president jim's  administration  he further told the delegates that  although their country was divided here  in south vietnam the militant march of  communism had been halted and from this  the free world derived great inspiration  [Music]  a visit to the honai refugee  resettlement area was another  opportunity for mr nixon to see the  extraordinary job vietnam is doing in  rehabilitating the hundreds of thousands  who fled the communist north  being a father himself he especially  enjoyed meeting the children  there were handshakes too for their  elders for the parents and the aged  who'd given up all they had to escape  communist tyranny and live in a land  that offered liberty  the vice president met the roman  catholic priests and others who had  worked long and selflessly to make this  camp a living and a growing thing  he saw new farm equipment made available  through united states assistance  he saw a deeply religious people  awaiting distribution of these tools  after their blessing by a bishop who was  a refugee from tonkin  it was an unforgettable experience for  the vice president  he felt he was witnessing here one of  the great stories of southeast asia the  story of south vietnam's fight for  freedom  and a better life for all  [Music]  on the evening of july 7th vice  president and mrs nixon arrived in  taiwan  at taipei airport u.s ambassador carl  rankin chinese vice president chen cheng  foreign minister george yay and premier  oku were among the first to welcome them  [Music]  also gathered to greet the personal  representative of president eisenhower  were important chinese government  officials and members of the diplomatic  corps  [Music]  the vice president's visit was  tremendously heartening to the capital  of nationalist china as evidenced by the  people's cheers  the next day he conferred with president  chiang kai-shek after giving him a  letter from president eisenhower a  letter that strongly reassured free  china's leader and his people of  steadfast u.s support in their stand  against communism with mrs nixon and  madame chang the two statesmen posed for  news cameraman  mr nixon summed up the content of their  talks in a brief statement  i just had a conference with president  xiang with regard to the relations  between the government of the united  states and the government of the  republic of china  and  in this conference we discussed the  mutual problems and the mutual dangers  which we confront in the world  the  government of the united states and the  people of the united states  stand firmly with the government of the  republic of china and the people of the  republic of china against communist  aggression in asia and other parts of  the world  as a result of this conference  i am sure that there will be no doubt  in the minds of people around the world  that our two countries and our two  peoples stand together against the  common danger which threatens three  people around the world the danger of  communist imperial colonialism  writing back to the airport the vice  president and his party were hailed by  thousands of chinese young and old who  lined the streets of  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  an taipei guard of honor was at the  airport to pay their country's respects  to the distinguished guests  these were crack contingents of  nationalist china's superbly trained  fighting forces  an unexpected delay caused by the  blowout of an airplane tire postponed  the departure  after further talks with chinese and u.s  officials vice president and mrs nixon  and planed for thailand six hours behind  schedule  it was close to midnight when they  arrived in bangkok  thai prime minister people son graham  and his wife greeted them with a  traditional charm for which their land  is famous  they were also greeted by u.s ambassador  max waldo bishop and other members of  the diplomatic corps  leading his guest into a reception room  at don muang airport the prime minister  added a formal welcome of his own  as foreign minister prince wan and other  dignitaries looked on the premier  recalled how warmly he had been received  during his own visit to america just a  year before  and he regretted that there wasn't time  enough to entertain his american friends  appropriately  in reply the us vice president insisted  that the welcome was much more than  adequate and he expressed deep  gratefulness that so many of thailand's  leaders should be kind enough to turn up  and meet him when he had been obliged to  come at so late an hour  in a dining room prepared for the  occasion the prime minister and mr nixon  and their party partook of a midnight  dinner  one more example of thailand's  hospitality to the american goodwill  ambassador  once again mr nixon held a press  conference he underscored america's  strong backing of the southeast asia  treaty organization  thailand and the united states he  declared will continue their efforts for  economic development and social  well-being so that all may pursue lives  in peace and happiness  then a last goodbye from prime minister  peeben sangram before his departure for  pakistan  [Music]  later that very morning his air force  transport reached karachi two of  pakistan's top cabinet members aya  chundraga and habib ibrahim rahim tula  welcomed the nixons to another seto  nation  the eighth punjab regiment provided the  honor guard  mr chundraga took the salute with vice  president nixon in the absence of prime  minister muhammad ali who was away in  london for the commonwealth conference  with the united states charged affair  arthur gardiner the vice president and  mrs nixon met more than 50 members of  the foreign diplomatic corps  karachi was another place that greatly  impressed mr nixon for tremendous  developments had taken place since last  he visited the capital  the cameraman accompanying the vice  president took these views of the city's  buildings  they are dramatic reminders of  pakistan's energy and determination  at the presidential palace mounted  rangers and white tunics provided a  colorful escort for the callers from the  united states  inside mr nixon posed with president  iskandar merza and cabaret ministers  after conferring with them on matters of  military and economic aid while mrs  nixon chatted with a charming and  gracious begum mercer  before leaving karachi the u.s vice  president granted another press  conference  on the question of kashmir he said the  united states hoped for a settlement by  direct india-pakistan talks or failing  that  through the united nations  it was at this press conference that he  warned countries of the free world  against accepting soviet aid the  communists price he said could be a rope  around their necks  unlike u.s assistance which is given to  maintain freedom aid from russia could  spell its end  after four hours in karachi the nixon's  plane departed swinging west toward the  capital of turkey  it was night when vice president and mrs  nixon arrived at ankara's snboa airport  both turkey's president jalal bayar and  her premier adnan menderes welcomed the  personal emissary of president  eisenhower mr bayar who recalled mr  nixon's hospitality when he himself  visited washington insisted that the  vice president and his wife be his  personal guests  [Music]  next day there were more of those chance  meetings with ordinary people a brief  chat with a young turkish news boy  brought out his deep interest in america  and his affection for president  eisenhower  these were feelings that mr nixon  noticed everywhere he went and in people  from widely different backgrounds and  national origins  [Music]  one of the most impressive moments of  vice president nixon's stay in the  turkish capital was his laying of a  wreath at the tomb of kemal ataturk the  founder of modern turkey  the second highest official of the  united states government paid a solemn  salute to the memory of one of the most  extraordinary leaders of this century  driving back to the city enroute to the  airport the american vice president's  motorcade stopped in the center of uluz  an ancient historic part of ankara  this gave mr nixon the opportunity once  more to meet the people he stepped out  and shook hands with many persons who  quickly surrounded his car  [Music]  at the airport there was one last  friendly touch  vice president and mrs nixon had a  personal goodbye for the police officers  who escorted them  a final farewell from the chief of the  national assembly and from premier  mandarin for whom mr nixon carried a  message to president eisenhower  then the vice president's plane turned  westward once more over the capital city  and over turkey that ancient land which  is at once so modern too  [Music]  at palma in the spanish mediterranean  island of mallorca the plane stopped  briefly to refuel vice president nixon  was greeted by the u.s ambassador to  spain john lodge and by spanish foreign  minister alberto martinez  last refueling stop was bermuda in the  mid-atlantic then 11 days after they had  left home vice president and mrs nixon  returned to washington  a large crowd greeted them including  senator noland and secretary of state  dulles  and historic tour was over  reassurances of east west friendship had  been made around the world especially  with six nations whom the united states  is proud to call partners in the  struggle for freedom  weekends on c-span 2

